This study systematically identifies the socioeconomic and technological determinants of agricultural carbon emissions in China, employing a multi-method analytical framework that integrates panel regression, machine learning algorithms, and scenario projection techniques. Drawing on provincial panel data spanning multiple decades, the analysis reveals that agricultural carbon emissions in China exhibit significant spatial heterogeneity, with major grain-producing regions contributing disproportionately to total emissions due to intensive input use. The decomposition of emission sources indicates that chemical fertilizers, agricultural film, and energy consumption for irrigation and machinery operation constitute the primary direct and indirect carbon sources within the agricultural sector.&nbsp;
<div>
 Socioeconomic determinants, including rural population density, per capita income levels, and the scale of agricultural output value, demonstrate robust positive associations with emission levels. Urbanization and the shift of rural labor to non-agricultural sectors show a moderating effect, suggesting that structural transformation can contribute to emission mitigation. Technological factors, particularly the adoption of precision farming techniques, soil carbon sequestration practices, and the efficiency of fertilizer application, emerge as significant negative determinants. The application of gradient boosting and deep learning models substantially improves the predictive accuracy of emission trajectories compared to traditional regression approaches, capturing nonlinear interactions among driving factors.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Scenario projections indicate that under a business-as-usual pathway, agricultural carbon emissions will continue to rise through 2035, driven primarily by the expansion of livestock production and the sustained use of synthetic inputs. However, a comprehensive mitigation scenario combining technological innovation, optimized input management, and policy interventions could achieve a peak in agricultural emissions by 2030, followed by a gradual decline. The findings underscore the critical importance of region-specific mitigation strategies, as emission determinants vary substantially across eastern, central, and western provinces due to differences in agricultural structure, economic development levels, and technological readiness.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The study concludes that achieving low-carbon agriculture in China requires a dual focus on socioeconomic restructuring and technological upgrading. Policies should prioritize the diffusion of low-carbon agricultural technologies, the promotion of circular agricultural practices, and the strengthening of carbon monitoring systems at the farm level. These insights provide an empirical basis for designing targeted emission reduction pathways that align with China’s broader carbon neutrality goals.&nbsp;
</div>

This study presents a comprehensive spatiotemporal analysis and forecasting framework for agricultural carbon emissions in China, integrating machine learning methodologies with panel data spanning multiple provinces from 2000 to 2022. Agricultural carbon emissions in China exhibit significant spatial heterogeneity, with high-emission clusters concentrated in the eastern plains and major grain-producing regions, while western and mountainous areas demonstrate substantially lower emission intensities. Temporal analysis reveals an overall upward trend in national agricultural carbon emissions, though the growth rate has decelerated since 2015, reflecting the impact of China’s agricultural绿色发展 policies and technological advancements in fertilizer efficiency and livestock management.&nbsp;
<div>
 To identify the key driving factors behind these emissions, a gradient boosting machine learning algorithm was employed, outperforming traditional panel regression models in capturing nonlinear relationships and interaction effects among variables. The analysis highlights that fertilizer application, livestock population density, agricultural machinery power, and irrigation area are the most influential predictors, collectively accounting for over 80% of the variance in provincial emission levels. Notably, the marginal effect of fertilizer use on emissions has diminished over time, suggesting diminishing returns from input intensification and the growing importance of efficiency improvements.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 For forecasting, a hybrid deep learning model combining convolutional neural networks with long short-term memory networks was developed to capture both spatial dependencies and temporal dynamics. The model was trained on historical provincial data and validated against out-of-sample observations, achieving a mean absolute percentage error below 5% for one-year&nbsp;ahead predictions. Scenario projections under three policy pathways—business-as-usual, moderate mitigation, and aggressive reduction—indicate that China’s agricultural carbon emissions could peak between 2027 and 2030 under current trends, but a 15–20% reduction from peak levels by 2035 is achievable only through coordinated interventions targeting fertilizer optimization, livestock waste management, and renewable energy adoption in agricultural machinery.
</div>
<div>
 The findings underscore the necessity of province-specific mitigation strategies, as the relative importance of emission drivers varies markedly across regions. For instance, in northeastern provinces, livestock emissions dominate, whereas in southern regions, fertilizer and irrigation are the primary contributors. This research provides a robust analytical foundation for policymakers to design targeted, data-driven interventions that align with China’s dual carbon goals, while demonstrating the superior predictive capability of machine learning approaches over conventional econometric methods in the context of complex, spatially heterogeneous agricultural systems.&nbsp;
</div>

This study investigates the relationship between crop carbon sink inequality and regional agricultural development through a multi-scale analysis of Hubei Province, China, from 1997 to 2022. Using panel data on crop yields, sown areas, and agricultural inputs across all prefecture-level administrative units, the research quantifies crop carbon sinks through a photosynthetic carbon fixation model and examines their spatial-temporal dynamics. The analysis reveals significant stage characteristics in carbon sink evolution, with an overall upward trend punctuated by periods of acceleration and stabilization, reflecting shifts in agricultural policies, technological adoption, and land use intensity.&nbsp;
<div>
 Spatial differences are pronounced: the Jianghan Plain and eastern hilly regions exhibit substantially higher carbon sink densities compared to the western mountainous areas, driven by variations in cropping structure, multiple cropping indices, and management practices. Decomposition of regional inequality using Theil index and kernel density estimation shows that intra-regional disparities have narrowed over time, while interregional gaps have persisted and, in some cases, widened, particularly between the central agricultural core and peripheral zones.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 This pattern suggests that crop carbon sink capacity is not only a biophysical outcome but also a product of uneven development trajectories, where access to irrigation infrastructure, fertilizer inputs, and market integration creates cumulative advantages for certain areas. The dynamic evolution analysis further indicates that high-sink regions tend to maintain their status, while low-sink regions face inertia in improving their carbon sequestration potential, pointing to path-dependent lock-in effects.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 From a regional development perspective, these inequalities raise critical questions about the distribution of both climate mitigation benefits and agricultural modernization gains. Areas with higher carbon sinks often correspond to more intensive farming systems that may entail trade-offs with other sustainability dimensions, such as water consumption, soil health, and biodiversity.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 The findings underscore the need for spatially differentiated policies that simultaneously enhance carbon sequestration in underperforming regions through agroecological practices, improved crop varieties, and extension services, while managing the environmental footprint of high-sink zones. The study contributes to the broader discourse on sustainable agricultural transformation by demonstrating that carbon sink inequality is a measurable and policy-relevant dimension of regional development disparities.&nbsp;
</div>
<div>
 Future research should integrate economic valuation of carbon services, explore telecoupling effects through trade flows, and assess the resilience of carbon sinks under climate change scenarios to inform more equitable and effective land-use planning.
</div>
